  "response": "Adjudicating between agency-centered and structure-centered explanations of a particular democratic breakdown begins with a careful statement of what each tradition actually claims. Juan Linz and Alfred Stepan's The Breakdown of Democratic Regimes emphasized the strategic choices of political leaders, while Milan Svolik's research links partisan polarization to voters' tolerance of undemocratic incumbents; a breakdown that turns on the calculations of leaders cannot also be one whose decisive permission comes from polarized voters, so these two locations of responsibility cannot both be correct. By contrast with this attention to what leaders decide and to what polarized electorates will excuse, structural accounts treat elite decisions as constrained by prevailing economic conditions. Carles Boix's Democracy and Redistribution, in arguing that economic inequality shapes the survival of democratic regimes, supplies the evidence that shows such constraint to be operative. Boix's verdict cannot be squared with Acemoglu and Robinson's Economic Origins of Dictatorship and Democracy, which concluded that economic inequality is irrelevant to regime transitions; one or the other of those two results must be mistaken.\n\nAdam Przeworski and his co-authors reported that no democracy with a per capita income above $6,055 had ever broken down, a finding that confirms the connection Seymour Martin Lipset's 1959 article drew between higher levels of economic development and democratic stability. How much weight such findings carry in any single case, however, depends on whether income thresholds are read as genuine ceilings on elite discretion or merely as a compact summary of past regularities.\n\nLevitsky and Ziblatt's How Democracies Die was published in 2008. Well after Lipset's 1959 intervention, Nancy Bermeo's 2016 Journal of Democracy article \"On Democratic Backsliding\" distinguished promissory coups from executive aggrandizement, giving analysts categories fine enough to specify what exactly stands in need of explanation. Later still, Stephan Haggard and Robert Kaufman's 2021 book Backsliding examines contemporary episodes of democratic erosion, and the sequence of these contributions marks a steady narrowing of the object of study.\n\nThe Varieties of Democracy team released the Episodes of Regime Transformation dataset to identify autocratization episodes. Freedom House publishes annual Freedom in the World scores for every country. Because autocratization episodes can now be delimited and dated one by one, research designs have been driven toward strategies that join statistical breadth to the close inspection of particular breakdowns. Evan Lieberman proposed nested analysis, which combines large-N statistical testing with small-N case studies. Giovanni Capoccia and Daniel Ziblatt called for a historical turn in the comparative study of democratization.\n\nThe upshot is that adjudication rarely ends in the outright victory of one tradition over the other. The competing accounts are more often assigned to different levels of the same explanation: economic conditions fix the range of outcomes available to a given polity, while the conduct of incumbents, oppositions and electorates determines which of those outcomes is realized. The decisive test, accordingly, is whether an account can identify work that its preferred variable does and the rival variable cannot, in the specific episode under dispute rather than in the abstract.",
